findings.requiredChange:
Compute a revision's uses the way the interleaving rule computes consumers of its bytes. Count, after the revision:
- a write or delegation consuming any holder of its bytes, directly or through read-only tool outputs;
- a model step consuming a holder directly, other than a revision of it;
- the delivery of a holder.
Take the needed gates from FAILs on any holder of a chain step's bytes. Skip revisions that produced nothing. Pin R601 and R604 (or R605) as refusal vectors and say the rule in the README. Checked: a sketch of exactly this on a copy of the round-6 model (WORK/alt) does the following:
- replays the 193 vectors unchanged, and the builder's isolation check still holds;
- leaves every round-1 to round-5 probe result unchanged;
- refuses R601, R602, R604 and R605, and accepts R606;
- also refuses R603, a cache-hit copy of r1's bytes delivered. That part (delivery of a holder) is optional, since 'Undeclared attempts' covers fresh steps;
- is linear per revision (see R6-4).

Apply the parent's FAIL to a delegated loop's steps as to its own. A child step holding the failed bytes is covered. A child step consuming what it covers is refused, except a read-only tool or a revision, and so is the child delivering it. Or state the limit under 'Not claimed'. Checked: a sketch on WORK/alt2 (about ten lines in _overridden: _tainted on the child's view for each DELEGATE after the FAIL whose child holds the failed bytes) does the following:
- replays the 193 vectors unchanged, and isolation still holds;
- leaves every round-1 to round-5 probe unchanged;
- refuses T604;
- accepts T604b, a child whose own gate fails the regenerated X and whose own revision passes (the in-loop analogue, round-5 C02, is accepted).
